pthreads: pthread_detach works, a detached thread frees its slot, join gives thread_sem back

pthread_detach was a stub that returned ESRCH, so std::thread::detach threw
"No such process". It now marks the thread detached; a detached thread
frees its slot when it ends (and is not waited for), and one that has ended
already frees it at once. A thread created detached does the same, where
before its slot was never given back.

pthread_join returned ESRCH and EINVAL with thread_sem still held, so the
next pthread call in any thread waited for ever.

pthread_create read the new thread's task after giving thread_sem back; a
detached thread that ended at once had freed its slot by then, and the
create said EAGAIN for a thread that had run. It reads it before.

Proven on AmigaOS 3.2 in a lab copy of Instance-32 with the stove's
qualification test t19 (1150 threads; 50 of them detached):
  stock:  "std::thread::detach threw: No such process", FAIL 50 detached
  0002:   PASS 50 detached threads; with -fuse-cxa-atexit (t19c), t19 OK
